{"title":"2015 AMC 10A Problems/Problem 14","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><div id=\"toc\" class=\"toc\" role=\"navigation\" aria-labelledby=\"mw-toc-heading\"><input type=\"checkbox\" role=\"button\" id=\"toctogglecheckbox\" class=\"toctogglecheckbox\" style=\"display:none\" /><div class=\"toctitle\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2 id=\"mw-toc-heading\">Contents</h2><span class=\"toctogglespan\"><label class=\"toctogglelabel\" for=\"toctogglecheckbox\"></label></span></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-1\"><a href=\"#Problem\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-2\"><a href=\"#Solution_1_(Important_Clarification)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 1 (Important Clarification)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-3\"><a href=\"#Solution_2\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 2</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-4\"><a href=\"#Solution_3\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">4</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 3</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-5\"><a href=\"#Solution_4\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">5</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 4</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-6\"><a href=\"#Solution_5\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">6</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 5</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-7\"><a href=\"#Solution_6\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">7</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 6</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-8\"><a href=\"#Solution_7\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">8</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 7</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-9\"><a href=\"#Solution_8\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">9</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 8</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-10\"><a href=\"#Solution_9\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">10</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 9</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-11\"><a href=\"#Solution_10_(Astronomy)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">11</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 10 (Astronomy)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-12\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_1\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">12</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution 1</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-13\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_2\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">13</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution 2</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-14\"><a href=\"#See_Also\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">14</span> <span class=\"toctext\">See Also</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2015_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_14&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>The diagram below shows the circular face of a clock with radius <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/8/3/f8366cd6196dd8a9da6d38a3e9eafb109e99d53e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$20$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"20\" height=\"14\" /> cm and a circular disk with radius <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/c/6/fc606f7f1e530731ab4f1cc364c01dc64a4455ee.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$10$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"20\" height=\"14\" /> cm externally tangent to the clock face at <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/d/f/edf074831eb5bc9e61d6d6e09f525a86e3068f6a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$12$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"20\" height=\"14\" /> o' clock. The disk has an arrow painted on it, initially pointing in the upward vertical direction. Let the disk roll clockwise around the clock face. At what point on the clock face will the disk be tangent when the arrow is next pointing in the upward vertical direction?\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/9/4/e94497740358c73cbf056b7bd46be244a502e289.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"[asy]size(170);defaultpen(linewidth(0.9)+fontsize(13pt));draw(unitcircle^^circle((0,1.5),0.5)); path arrow = origin--(-0.13,-0.35)--(-0.06,-0.35)--(-0.06,-0.7)--(0.06,-0.7)--(0.06,-0.35)--(0.13,-0.35)--cycle; for(int i=1;i&lt;=12;i=i+1){draw(0.9*dir(90-30*i)--dir(90-30*i));label(&quot;$&quot;+(string) i+&quot;$&quot;,0.78*dir(90-30*i));} dot(origin);draw(shift((0,1.87))*arrow);draw(arc(origin,1.5,68,30),EndArrow(size=12));[/asy]\" width=\"218\" height=\"285\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/a/1/4a118e3faa0e239a1e4a9e608f96e7181c9f7a7a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\textbf{(A) }\\text{2 o&#39; clock} \\qquad\\textbf{(B) }\\text{3 o&#39; clock} \\qquad\\textbf{(C) }\\text{4 o&#39; clock} \\qquad\\textbf{(D) }\\text{6 o&#39; clock} \\qquad\\textbf{(E) }\\text{8 o&#39; clock}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"651\" height=\"17\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_1_.28Important_Clarification.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_1_(Important_Clarification)\">Solution 1 (Important Clarification)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2015_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_14&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 1 (Important Clarification)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>The below solutions don't do a great job on telling you why the answer is not <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/8/c/f8c7d61b85cb8ade83f23b7ebb8e3980c4c2711f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\textbf{(D) }\\text{6 o&#39; clock}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"106\" height=\"18\" />. For those who don't understand it, the below video is great on explaining it.\n</p><p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=FUHkTs-Ipfg&amp;t=612s\">The SAT question everyone got wrong</a>\n</p><p>Skip to 10:13.\n</p><p>Here is the best approach to the problem, and any problem of this manner.\n</p><p>If you had watched the video (highly recommended), you should have learned that when a circle <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/9/019e9892786e493964e145e7c5cf7b700314e53b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" /> travels along the outside of any figure <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/f/5/ff5fb3d775862e2123b007eb4373ff6cc1a34d4e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$B$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"17\" height=\"14\" />, the total number of rotations circle <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/9/019e9892786e493964e145e7c5cf7b700314e53b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" /> made along the revolution of the figure is just the perimeter <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/2/6/426669a11d8d99e093e31e07312c3bacb7a85f56.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P_B$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"21\" height=\"15\" /> plus the circumference <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/3/9/c399ad774aa091062a1b7cf70c4c975b314fde82.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$C_A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"26\" height=\"17\" /> divided by <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/3/9/c399ad774aa091062a1b7cf70c4c975b314fde82.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$C_A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"26\" height=\"17\" />, or <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/6/a/86a91672472ac56cf9798d96902032b0050fd55f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{P_B + C_A}{C_A}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -16px\" width=\"75\" height=\"42\" />. (This is proved in the video as well).\n</p><p>Therefore, the total number of rotations of the smaller circle rotating around the clock is the circumference of the clock, which is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/5/3/d53f4262ddb348a79531d8dc668932b544437f30.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$40\\pi$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"31\" height=\"14\" />, plus the circumference of the smaller circle, which is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/c/f/9cf5d779e3740a6916b73852f858ab4a72c82e92.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$20\\pi$\" width=\"28\" height=\"12\" />, divided by the circumference of the smaller circle, which is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/c/f/9cf5d779e3740a6916b73852f858ab4a72c82e92.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$20\\pi$\" width=\"28\" height=\"12\" />. Then, we see that the arrow rotates a total of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/7/c/f7cf845aa3798e8abd8fd80a4ef67b78902c4a47.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{40\\pi + 20\\pi}{20\\pi} \\Rightarrow \\frac{60\\pi}{20\\pi} \\Rightarrow \\frac{60}{20} \\Rightarrow 3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"236\" height=\"38\" />.\n</p><p>Finally, because the clock is marked at 12 intervals, each rotation is at every <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/f/a/afa276c1aa2f343298162fb86176a4cd13219a4e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$12/3 = 4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"72\" height=\"20\" /> hour intervals.\n</p><p>Therefore, if the circle starts at 12:00, then it will make its first full rotation at <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/7/dc79a4029bc276aa6f6c2c9b994ff6d42778718e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{\\textbf{(C) }4 \\ \\text{o&#39; clock}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -10px\" width=\"116\" height=\"28\" />, not <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/8/c/f8c7d61b85cb8ade83f23b7ebb8e3980c4c2711f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\textbf{(D) }\\text{6 o&#39; clock}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"106\" height=\"18\" />.\n</p><p>I hope this cleared all confusion. It is important to understand these topics to avoid mistakes on later tests.\n</p><p>~Pinotation\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_2\">Solution 2</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2015_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_14&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 2\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>The circumference of the clock is twice that of the disk. So, a quarter way around the clock (3:00), the point halfway around the disk will be tangent. The arrow will point to the left. We can see the disk made a 75% rotation from 12 to 3, and 3 is 75% of 4, so it would make 100% rotation from 12 to 4. The answer is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/7/dc79a4029bc276aa6f6c2c9b994ff6d42778718e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{\\textbf{(C) }4 \\ \\text{o&#39; clock}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -10px\" width=\"116\" height=\"28\" />. Similarly, the arrow would be pointing downward at 6:00. It would already have completed three 180 degree turns. Therefore, two 180 degree turns would be completed at 4:00.\n</p><p><br />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_3\">Solution 3</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2015_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_14&amp;action=edit&amp;section=4\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 3\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>The rotation factor of the arrow is the sum of the rates of the regular rotation of the arrow (360° every 360° rotation = 1) and the rotation of the disk around the clock with twice the circumference (360° every 180° = 2). Thus, the rotation factor of the arrow is 3, and so our answer corresponds to 360°/3 = 120°, which is 4 o' clock. <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/7/dc79a4029bc276aa6f6c2c9b994ff6d42778718e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{\\textbf{(C) }4 \\ \\text{o&#39; clock}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -10px\" width=\"116\" height=\"28\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_4\">Solution 4</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2015_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_14&amp;action=edit&amp;section=5\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 4\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>The arrow travels a path of radius 30 (20 from the interior clock and 10 from the radius of the disk itself). We note that 1 complete rotation of 360 degrees is needed for the arrow to appear up again, so, therefore, the disk must travel its circumference before the arrow goes up. Its circumference is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/c/f/9cf5d779e3740a6916b73852f858ab4a72c82e92.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$20\\pi$\" width=\"28\" height=\"12\" />, so that is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/c/f/9cf5d779e3740a6916b73852f858ab4a72c82e92.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$20\\pi$\" width=\"28\" height=\"12\" /> traveled on a <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/5/3/853d147eb41b0b558ab393d6095d6f1d2fe2ebc5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$60\\pi$\" width=\"28\" height=\"12\" /> arrow path. This is a ratio of 1/3, so the angle it carves is 120 degrees, which leads us to the correct answer of  4 o' clock. <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/7/dc79a4029bc276aa6f6c2c9b994ff6d42778718e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{\\textbf{(C) }4 \\ \\text{o&#39; clock}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -10px\" width=\"116\" height=\"28\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_5\">Solution 5</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2015_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_14&amp;action=edit&amp;section=6\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 5\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Suppose that the small disk also had a clock face on it, and that both disks were toothed wheels, free to rotate around their centers. The part of the picture where they engage would look like this:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/d/b/edb7ba117a992d5ef3e752ab4bd95997e69b782f.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"[asy] fill(arc((0,0),2,30,150)--cycle,lightgrey); draw(arc((0,0),2,30,150)); draw(1.8*dir(90)--2*dir(90)); draw(1.8*dir(60)--2*dir(60)); label(&quot;12&quot;,1.56*dir(90)); label(&quot;1&quot;,1.56*dir(60));  draw(arc((0,3),1,-15,-165)); draw(0.9*dir(-90)+(0,3)--dir(-90)+(0,3)); draw(0.9*dir(-60)+(0,3)--dir(-60)+(0,3)); draw(0.9*dir(-30)+(0,3)--dir(-30)+(0,3)); label(&quot;6&quot;,.74*dir(-90)+(0,3)); label(&quot;5&quot;,.74*dir(-60)+(0,3)); label(&quot;4&quot;,.74*dir(-30)+(0,3)); [/asy]\" width=\"252\" height=\"128\" />\nThe small cog has half the radius, and therefore half the circumference. If the large cog turns <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/1/9/919bf5d461970fc8d905d63704ef763c08594184.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$30^\\circ$\" width=\"24\" height=\"13\" /> counterclockwise (i.e. 1 hour), the small cog turns <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/9/f/29fa0a9949f018e121b2093216dd5db13d3abef0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$60^\\circ$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"28\" height=\"15\" /> clockwise (i.e. 2 hours).\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/5/b/c5bb20418b1cb61491fec2be43b03f98a45bb1d7.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"[asy] fill(arc((0,0),2,30,150)--cycle,lightgrey); draw(arc((0,0),2,30,150),EndArcArrow);label(&quot;$30^\\circ$&quot;,2*dir(150),W); draw(1.8*dir(120)--2*dir(120)); draw(1.8*dir(90)--2*dir(90)); label(rotate(30)*&quot;12&quot;,1.56*dir(120)); label(rotate(30)*&quot;1&quot;,1.56*dir(90));  draw(arc((0,3),1,-15,-165),EndArcArrow);label(&quot;$60^\\circ$&quot;,dir(-165)+(0,3),W); draw(0.9*dir(-90)+(0,3)--dir(-90)+(0,3)); draw(0.9*dir(-120)+(0,3)--dir(-120)+(0,3)); draw(0.9*dir(-150)+(0,3)--dir(-150)+(0,3)); label(rotate(-60)*&quot;6&quot;,.74*dir(-150)+(0,3)); label(rotate(-60)*&quot;5&quot;,.74*dir(-120)+(0,3)); label(rotate(-60)*&quot;4&quot;,.74*dir(-90)+(0,3)); [/asy]\" width=\"252\" height=\"128\" />\nHowever, in the original problem the large cog does not rotate; it stays where it is. Therefore we must turn the whole diagram above <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/1/9/919bf5d461970fc8d905d63704ef763c08594184.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$30^\\circ$\" width=\"24\" height=\"13\" /> clockwise to see what happens when the small cog rolls around it.\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/6/f/66f374481039a9d96d11c461d779d2c28b68a895.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"[asy] fill(arc((0,0),2,30,150)--cycle,lightgrey); draw(arc((0,0),2,30,150)); draw(1.8*dir(90)--2*dir(90)); draw(1.8*dir(60)--2*dir(60)); label(&quot;12&quot;,1.56*dir(90)); label(&quot;1&quot;,1.56*dir(60));  pair c=(1.5,sqrt(27)/2); draw(arc(c,1,0,-200),EndArcArrow);label(&quot;$90^\\circ$&quot;,dir(-180)+c,W); draw(0.9*dir(-120)+c--dir(-120)+c); draw(0.9*dir(-150)+c--dir(-150)+c); draw(0.9*dir(-180)+c--dir(-180)+c); label(rotate(-90)*&quot;6&quot;,.74*dir(-180)+c); label(rotate(-90)*&quot;5&quot;,.74*dir(-150)+c); label(rotate(-90)*&quot;4&quot;,.74*dir(-120)+c); [/asy]\" width=\"252\" height=\"118\" />\nIt turns out that, when the point of tangency moves <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/1/9/919bf5d461970fc8d905d63704ef763c08594184.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$30^\\circ$\" width=\"24\" height=\"13\" /> clockwise (one hour), from our point of view the small disk rotates <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/2/e/22ec0d2e4202839322d26a23dcb13f79821df5d7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$90^\\circ$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"28\" height=\"15\" /> clockwise (three hours) around its center. Thus, for the small disk to perform a complete rotation of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/f/9/cf9506d61661a7d046ee93b381ddb704684ffd15.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$360^\\circ$\" width=\"33\" height=\"13\" /> (twelve hours) around its center from our point of view, the point of tangency must move round four hours. So the answer is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/7/dc79a4029bc276aa6f6c2c9b994ff6d42778718e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{\\textbf{(C) }4 \\ \\text{o&#39; clock}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -10px\" width=\"116\" height=\"28\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_6\">Solution 6</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2015_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_14&amp;action=edit&amp;section=7\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 6\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>We unfold the big clock and draw the small clock. We know that the small clocks arrow will be facing up again at 6 o clock on the unfolded big clock. However, because of the coin rotation paradox, the small clock actually needs to rotate thrice and not twice to go all the way around the big clock, so the answer would be <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/7/dc79a4029bc276aa6f6c2c9b994ff6d42778718e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{\\textbf{(C) }4 \\ \\text{o&#39; clock}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -10px\" width=\"116\" height=\"28\" />. We now draw corresponding arrows at each hour and fold it back again.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_7\">Solution 7</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2015_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_14&amp;action=edit&amp;section=8\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 7\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>We can approach this problem with angle measures. As the circumference of the disk is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/e/9/4e98c51e58fe2f353a6811264d4cac079b7ad5bf.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$10\\pi,$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"33\" height=\"16\" /> and the clock <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/e/8/fe8f3fb7f6b71d4ade5323708fad26880cb0034b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$20 \\pi,$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"33\" height=\"16\" /> we have that 30 degrees, or the angular measure between hours, of the disk is only 15 degrees of the clock. This yields that every two hour ticks that the clock rotates, on the third one, the ticks will meet. However, the disk must rotate 360 degrees in order to come back to its original position, so the angular measure that the disk has covered relative to the clock is simply <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/0/4/6047f3fd6644adbdf5c4059ef2402ba64280bbb3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$12 \\cdot 15 \\cdot \\frac{2}{3},$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"79\" height=\"37\" /> or <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/3/f/f3fd424e953ac76b50bf6103875acc049d21d3c2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$120^\\circ$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"33\" height=\"13\" /> from the 12 starting point, so <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/7/dc79a4029bc276aa6f6c2c9b994ff6d42778718e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{\\textbf{(C) }4 \\ \\text{o&#39; clock}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -10px\" width=\"116\" height=\"28\" />.\n</p><p>~ab2024\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_8\">Solution 8</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2015_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_14&amp;action=edit&amp;section=9\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 8\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>If the big clock were a flat plane, then the smaller clock could travel <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/8/6/b869eced65278c414ded7b9ae21fd60c255b67f0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\dfrac{40\\pi}{20\\pi}=2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"68\" height=\"38\" /> full revolutions.\n</p><p>But we also need to account for rotation. If we mark a red dot on the bottom of the small clock/bottom of the arrow, and then drag it around the clock, the direction of the arrow would still change. After traveling around the big clock, the small clock would travel <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> full rotation.\n</p><p>Considering these two movements, the small clock travels 3 full rotations around the big clock so the arrow is next pointing upwards at <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/c/2/3c2fa57402e4b0b941e1132a26afed4c04083708.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\dfrac{12 \\text{ o&#39;clock}}{3}=\\boxed{\\textbf{(C) }4 \\ \\text{o&#39; clock}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"219\" height=\"38\" />.\n</p><p>~BakedPotato66\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_9\">Solution 9</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2015_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_14&amp;action=edit&amp;section=10\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 9\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>The center of rotation is in the center of the smaller circle, but extends to the center of the larger circle. That means the circumference of the circle in relation to the arrow is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/8/5/f8545123b36df7206dd273b0012b9f062f4f7b65.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$60 \\pi$\" width=\"28\" height=\"12\" />. The other circle is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/d/1/ad1b502ff80a0471cf3dd21e6fb7dd9bee7fad6c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$20 \\pi$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"31\" height=\"14\" /> and so that is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/e/1/4e1965f8d70832ded66434c75a7bd52b75090af0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{1}{3}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"15\" height=\"38\" />. So <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/3/5/235820ed89f9ed54954565f6f7e529e091874bde.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{12}{3} = 4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"55\" height=\"37\" /> which is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/7/dc79a4029bc276aa6f6c2c9b994ff6d42778718e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{\\textbf{(C) }4 \\ \\text{o&#39; clock}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -10px\" width=\"116\" height=\"28\" />.\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_10_.28Astronomy.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_10_(Astronomy)\">Solution 10 (Astronomy)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2015_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_14&amp;action=edit&amp;section=11\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 10 (Astronomy)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Let the center of the disk be Planet X with orbit eccentricity <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/c/1/bc1f9d9bf8a1b606a4188b5ce9a2af1809e27a89.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> and the center of the clock be the Sun. Note that the question would then be asking for the solar day of Planet X, rather than the sidereal day. Because planet X is rotating around its axis in the same direction as it is revolving around the Sun, the solar day can be calculated as <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/9/0/2909be68d3fc8a272191a4d3d6ac44c868b0c1e9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$d_{solar}=\\frac{1}{\\frac{1}{y}+\\frac{1}{d_{siderial}}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -22px\" width=\"159\" height=\"47\" />, where <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/9/2/092e364e1d9d19ad5fffb0b46ef4cc7f2da02c1c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" /> is the length of the year of Planet X and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/1/6/816e0d54e11321cc411d4b18ec6533b6f9657e35.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$d_{siderial}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"59\" height=\"17\" /> is one sidereal day. It is easy to see that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/5/2/c52f2821f1a68dc474c90d7acb0e279c7fca51c6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y=12$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"51\" height=\"15\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/4/c/74c9198a93fd695d4af961917e8786f26d887fa9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$d_{siderial}=6$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"92\" height=\"17\" />, therefore the answer is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/3/c/e3ce220a73e5db7ec899fe13e3356bee6789a349.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{1}{\\frac{1}{12}+\\frac{1}{6}}=\\frac{1}{\\frac{1}{4}}=\\boxed{\\textbf{(C) }4 \\ \\text{o&#39; clock}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -20px\" width=\"235\" height=\"45\" />\n~That one kid\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution_1\">Video Solution 1</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2015_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_14&amp;action=edit&amp;section=12\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution 1\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://youtu.be/hPEgOYA4Xq8\">https://youtu.be/hPEgOYA4Xq8</a>\n</p><p>~savannahsolver\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution_2\">Video Solution 2</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2015_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_14&amp;action=edit&amp;section=13\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution 2\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Video Solution: <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=kCsQbTi3iLU\">https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=kCsQbTi3iLU</a>\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"See_Also\">See Also</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2015_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_14&amp;action=edit&amp;section=14\" title=\"Edit section: See Also\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>2014 AIME I #10: <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/index.php/2014_AIME_I_Problems/Problem_10\">https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/index.php/2014_AIME_I_Problems/Problem_10</a>\n</p><p>Coin Rotation Paradox: <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Coin_rotation_paradox\">https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Coin_rotation_paradox</a>\n</p><p>A related problem from the SAT in 1982 (see Coin Rotation Paradox above)\nThis gives a more rigorous proof by looking at relative velocities and showing that the distance the center of the disk travels equals the total distance the disk rotates.\n<a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=FUHkTs-Ipfg\">https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=FUHkTs-Ipfg</a>\n(w veritasium)\n</p>\n<table class=\"wikitable\" style=\"margin:0.5em auto; font-size:95%; border:1px solid black; width:80%;\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td style=\"background:#ccf;text-align:center;\" colspan=\"3\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2015_AMC_10A\" title=\"2015 AMC 10A\">2015 AMC 10A</a></b> (<b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2015_AMC_10A_Problems\" title=\"2015 AMC 10A Problems\">Problems</a></b> • <b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2015_AMC_10A_Answer_Key\" title=\"2015 AMC 10A Answer Key\">Answer Key</a></b> • <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/community/c13\">Resources</a>)\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Preceded&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2015_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_13\" title=\"2015 AMC 10A Problems/Problem 13\">Problem 13</a></b>\n</td>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Followed&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2015_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_15\" title=\"2015 AMC 10A Problems/Problem 15\">Problem 15</a></b>\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"3\" style=\"text-align:center;\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2015_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_1\" title=\"2015 AMC 10A Problems/Problem 1\">1</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2015_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_2\" title=\"2015 AMC 10A Problems/Problem 2\">2</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2015_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_3\" title=\"2015 AMC 10A Problems/Problem 3\">3</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2015_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_4\" title=\"2015 AMC 10A Problems/Problem 4\">4</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2015_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_5\" title=\"2015 AMC 10A Problems/Problem 5\">5</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2015_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_6\" title=\"2015 AMC 10A Problems/Problem 6\">6</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2015_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_7\" title=\"2015 AMC 10A Problems/Problem 7\">7</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2015_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_8\" title=\"2015 AMC 10A Problems/Problem 8\">8</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2015_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_9\" title=\"2015 AMC 10A Problems/Problem 9\">9</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2015_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_10\" title=\"2015 AMC 10A Problems/Problem 10\">10</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2015_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_11\" title=\"2015 AMC 10A Problems/Problem 11\">11</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2015_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_12\" title=\"2015 AMC 10A Problems/Problem 12\">12</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2015_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_13\" title=\"2015 AMC 10A Problems/Problem 13\">13</a> <b>•</b> <a class=\"mw-selflink selflink\">14</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2015_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_15\" title=\"2015 AMC 10A Problems/Problem 15\">15</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2015_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_16\" title=\"2015 AMC 10A Problems/Problem 16\">16</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2015_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_17\" title=\"2015 AMC 10A Problems/Problem 17\">17</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2015_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_18\" title=\"2015 AMC 10A Problems/Problem 18\">18</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2015_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_19\" title=\"2015 AMC 10A Problems/Problem 19\">19</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2015_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_20\" title=\"2015 AMC 10A Problems/Problem 20\">20</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2015_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_21\" title=\"2015 AMC 10A Problems/Problem 21\">21</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2015_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_22\" title=\"2015 AMC 10A Problems/Problem 22\">22</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2015_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_23\" title=\"2015 AMC 10A Problems/Problem 23\">23</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2015_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_24\" title=\"2015 AMC 10A Problems/Problem 24\">24</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2015_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_25\" title=\"2015 AMC 10A Problems/Problem 25\">25</a>\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"3\" style=\"text-align:center;\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=AMC_10_Problems_and_Solutions\" title=\"AMC 10 Problems and Solutions\"> All AMC 10 Problems and Solutions</a></b>\n</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<div class=\"print\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;width:100%;justify-content:space-between;align-items:center\">\n<div>\n<p><span class=\"mw-default-size\" typeof=\"mw:File\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=File:AMC_Logo.png\" class=\"mw-file-description\"><img src=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/d/d1/AMC_Logo.png\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"97\" height=\"84\" class=\"mw-file-element\" /></a></span>\n</p>\n</div>\n<div style=\"flex-grow:1;text-align:right\">\n<p>These problems are copyrighted &#169; by the <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://www.maa.org\">Mathematical Association of America</a